                    "s1_title" => "You are relaxed, warm, and welcoming. You always tend to be a source of connection and harmony.",
                    "s1_content" => "
                        <p>You have the strength of being able to see from every point of view, a natural mediator. Putting yourself in the shoes of others you bring connection where connection seems to be lost. </p>
                        <p>However, this is a constant burden you feel you must carry. You believe it is your responsibility to ensure connection in the world which creates a natural fear and avoidance of conflict. Yes, you fear conflict, but it is what conflict brings that you fear most, a perceived loss of connection. However, many others believe that conflict IS connection. Now what? In a world full of conflict and disagreement your inner stability and comfort is always at risk. To ensure others are at peace and to avoid conflict you numb your own desires, dreams, and feelings. Hence, the slothful dominant struggle. You “go along to get along.”</p>
                        <p>Numbing your desires is also harmful as it reinforces the thought that your voice and presence are not as important as others. You want to be heard and seen but you struggle with inserting yourself. Feeling like it is too much trouble to make yourself seen and heard you fall asleep to your passions and withdraw to keep the peace, internally and externally. </p>
                        <p>Your attempts to keep the peace inevitably backfire as those around you get frustrated at your passivity, stubbornness, emotional unavailability, and passive-aggressive responses. This results in the very thing you were seeking to avoid, conflict.</p>
                        <p class='mb-0'>However, at your best, you become fully aware of your passions and desires as you “wake up” and realize the only way out is through. You know and believe that you matter and are built to make a difference in this world. You finally understand that true peace comes from entering into conflict, not avoiding it, you genuinely engage with others and life. You bring people together, embrace purpose, and finally achieve true harmony.</p>
